38 files contain `legacy_auth` (including `app/auth.py` itself, which must keep the definition). So 37 files need migration. The structure is highly uniform — every handler follows the pattern:

```python
def handle_N(user_id, [payload]) -> dict:
    if not legacy_auth(user_id):
        return {} / raise PermissionError('user_id')
    return {'ok': True} / {'created': True, 'data': payload} / {'updated': True, ...}
```

The task: replace `legacy_auth(<user>)` with `new_auth(<user>, scope="read")` unless the surrounding code does a write/mutation (look for nearby `create_`, `update_`, `delete_`, `save_`, `write_`, or `POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH` HTTP verbs in the same function).

The heuristic: "If the surrounding code clearly does a write/mutation ... in the same function". So I need to look at each function's body. Many files return `{'ok': True}` — read. Others return `{'created': True, 'data': payload}` — write (create). `{'updated': True}` — write (update). Let me read all the files fully to determine scope for each call site.

